From tru64-unix-managers  Fri Mar 06 04:26:34 1998
From: "David B. Ritch" <dbritch () earthlink ! net>
Date: Fri, 06 Mar 1998 04:26:34 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: Summary: Strange nfs problem when delivering mail
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96078701111297

Thanks to Ben Goodwin, Steve VanDevender, and Tim McCarthy for responses on
this.  The consensus was that this is a locking problem.  I tried several
things, and finally moved the mail spool to a local raid filesystem on my
DEC mail server.  This eliminated the stale nfs file handle errors (of
course).

However, I am still seeing a problem.  Many times each day, binmail reports
via syslog that it is waiting for a lock.  A few times each day, it reports
that it is overriding a lock.  It appears to be dropping mail at least some
of the times that it reports waiting for a lock but not overriding it.  It
looks like binmail is quietly giving up and not delivering the mail.

Any ideas why?

Thanks,

David Ritch

-----Original Message-----
From: David B. Ritch <dbritch@earthlink.net>
To: Alpha Managers List <alpha-osf-managers@ornl.gov>
Date: Thursday, February 19, 1998 9:48 PM
Subject: Strange nfs problem when delivering mail


>My site is using Berkeley Sendmail version 8.8.7 on our mail exchanger,
>using binmail for local delivery.  Our mail spool is nfs mounted on the
>server from a Network Appliance, which has been extremely reliable.  We are
>seeing an occasional problem that has me baffled.  A few times a day,
>binmail is reporting a failure while fclosing a file.  It claims to be
>getting nfs error 70 - stale nfs file handle.
>
>The file handle is *not* stale.  The filesystem continues to be mounted
>throughout the day.  At the suggestion of a Network Appliance engineer, we
>switched from nfs 3.0 to 2.0, and are mounting the filesystem through
>/etc/fstab instead of automount.  However, the problem continues.
>
>Does anyone have a suggestion of what might be causing this?
>
>The mail exchange host is a DEC 4000 running DU 4.0B, and the NetApp is an
>F540 running DataOntap 4.3 (and had the same problems under 4.2).
>
>Thanks in advance for any help!
>
>David Ritch
